A||||10||Kwez||88GT braking perfo||||5||
Z||000000||Kwez||09-16-2001||03:36 PM||||hi all.<BR>Anybody have real performance numbers for 60-0 braking for an 88GT ??<P>That thread ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013648.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013648.html</A>  ) about 60-0 in 186ft is drivin' me nuts !<P>People usually say "88 brakes are plenty good" ... but to me 0-60 in 186ft is actually 'scarry bad'.<P>I have basically stock 88 brakes w. performance (KVR) pads. Someone from the Mtl Fiero Club has the same setup & says it brakes very hard ...<P>So, anybody has done some tests ?<P>thx<BR>Philippe||||reg||5
Z||000001||ray b||09-16-2001||04:09 PM||||vented disks fade less on repeated use.<BR>driver skill pads and overall condishion<BR>may be reason for poor distance on first stop.<BR>tyres grip is a factor too.<BR>bigger brakes are needed esp with swaps of biger/heavy motors.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-16-2001||04:26 PM||||MW got 118 ft from 55-0. Depening on todays pad and tire changes, the numbers might even be lower. My Formula sure feels like it could hit lower than 118ft. But the organic pads I have on it are wicked sticky for daily use. I don't think they'd like heavy track use, but I don't auto-x so haven't seen them overheat yet.<P>PS. brakes don't stop cars, tires do. If you can lock 'em up with stock setup "hi-po" pads won't lower your numbers.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||09-16-2001||06:08 PM||||No exact numbers, but I can speak from first hand experience with the KVR pads. When everything is new and fresh, braking performance is excellent, with little fade, good control and the fluid does not boil. The problem is that the aluminum on steel sliding mechanism tends to seize up in hard use, and develops lots of play that leads to a long pedal. After about half of a season, I'm not happy with the '88 caliper set up and plan to dump it. This is only for track use, mind you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||09-16-2001||06:27 PM||||Intresting, I'm fed up with the unreliable 88 calipers as well.  But this is in two seperate street cars (admitedly, I drive them like they're meant to be, but neither car sees much total track time at all...)<P>Still, I've had it, and I'm pulling basically the entire system off a far better equiped car. (hopefully with defeatable ABS...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B>No exact numbers, but I can speak from first hand experience with the KVR pads. When everything is new and fresh, braking performance is excellent, with little fade, good control and the fluid does not boil. The problem is that the aluminum on steel sliding mechanism tends to seize up in hard use, and develops lots of play that leads to a long pedal. After about half of a season, I'm not happy with the '88 caliper set up and plan to dump it. This is only for track use, mind you.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||montage||09-17-2001||08:14 AM||||I have a 88 Formula and using a G-tech with stock brakes my best 60-0 is 130'. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||09-17-2001||08:28 AM||||I have a G-Tech too. I haven't tried too many times (2-3) but my best is 159 ft wich I think is bad for my car. I have braided lines and I think carbomet pads. Maybe they need to heat up more. 130 ft with stock brakes is pretty impressive. All magazine test that I have seen (Brooklands compilation) have 88s about equal with pre-88s around 160 ft 60-0. The worst if I remember well were 84s.||||reg||1
